From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: spice-mac <spice-mac@localhost>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 09:08:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 07/21] display: play video out soon after arrival when no
 audio is playing
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

spice-server stamps video frames with their capture time but keeps the client's
clock MM_TIME_DELTA (400 ms) behind its own, so video can be lined up with
buffered audio, and only ever raises that latency. With no audio playing that is
400 ms of pure lag on every streamed region — video, but also windows dragged or
scrolled under streaming-video=all.

Unless the audio is in charge, play video out against the channel's own clock
(monotonic time, started from the session's), with frame times shifted so that
frames are shown SPICE_STREAM_PLAYOUT_MS (default 50) after arrival, keeping
their relative timing: the shift is measured from the smallest slack seen, drops
at once if a frame would be late, grows back at most 5 ms a second, and is
measured again should the slack exceed the target by a second (the server's
clock moved). The own clock ignores the session's, which is yanked forward when
the audio backend reports its delay and back when the server re-sends its clock
at the end of playback: frames queued against it would be dropped or held each
time.

The audio is in charge once the backend has reported its delay (the playback
channel now forgets that delay when playback restarts) and playback has lasted
2 s more, some 3-4 s into a sound: video then follows the session clock and the
server's timestamps, for lip sync, after one pause of about the audio delay.
Shorter sounds, such as notifications, leave the video's timing alone. When the
audio stops, the own clock continues from the session's and the shift is
measured again: frames still queued then have later times than the next one and
the decoder drops them, a one-off skip. Stream reports and statistics keep the
session clock and unshifted margins. 0 disables all of this.

Measured click-to-pixel into the client's framebuffer (headless spice-glib
client over an ssh tunnel on a LAN; Windows 11 guest, MJPEG, 1280x720 at 30 fps;
21 samples each, median): 483 ms with this off, 128 / 154 / 185 ms at
30 / 50 / 80. Frame delivery and its jitter were the same in all runs. With a
2 s beep every 8 s: no decoder queue resets and a longest pause between shown
frames of 112 ms, against 4 resets and 244 ms when the playout followed the
session clock.

Also log, once a second, the pacing of the frames actually shown and how late
they were shown on the playout clock, and log the playout transitions.
